## Steps to Reproduce

# 1. Below is my code.

```
class ReorderableListDemoView extends StatefulWidget {
  @override
  _ReorderableListDemoViewState createState() => _ReorderableListDemoViewState();
}

class _ReorderableListDemoViewState extends State<ReorderableListDemoView> {

  List<Widget> _widgetList;

  defText(String text) {
    return Text(
      text,
      style: TextStyle(
        fontSize: 15,
        color: Colors.black,
        decoration: TextDecoration.none,
      ),
    );
  }

  @override
  void initState() {
    
    _widgetList = List.generate(8, (index) => 
      Container(
        key: ValueKey('$index'),
        height: 55,
        color: Color(Random().nextInt(0xFFFFFFFF)),
        alignment: Alignment.center,
        child: defText('$index'),
      )
    );

    super.initState();
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return SafeArea(
      child: Container(
        color: Colors.white,
        child: Column(
          children: <Widget>[
            Container(
              height: 100,
              alignment: Alignment.center,
              child: defText('Can try to drag.'),
            ),
            Expanded(
              child: ReorderableListView(
                  children: _widgetList,
                  onReorder: onReorder,
                ),
            ),
          ],
        ),
      ),
    );
  }

  onReorder(int oldIndex, int newIndex) {
    print('oldIndex: $oldIndex newIndex: $newIndex');

    int direction = oldIndex < newIndex ? 1 : -1;
    var temp = _widgetList[oldIndex];
    var check = (index) => (newIndex - index).abs() + (oldIndex - index).abs() == (newIndex - oldIndex).abs(); 
    for (int i = oldIndex; check(i); i += direction) {
      _widgetList[i] = _widgetList[i + direction];
    }
    _widgetList[newIndex] = temp; 
  }

}

```

# 2. Try to drag item: 1 -> 3, 7 -> 5

There will be movement animation during the dragging process, but in fact it will not change the order of the items in the list.
In onReorder callback, change the order of list items.

```
onReorder(int oldIndex, int newIndex) {
  print('oldIndex: $oldIndex newIndex: $newIndex');

  int direction = oldIndex < newIndex ? 1 : -1;
  var temp = _widgetList[oldIndex];
  var check = (index) => (newIndex - index).abs() + (oldIndex - index).abs() == (newIndex - oldIndex).abs(); 
  for (int i = oldIndex; check(i); i += direction) {
    _widgetList[i] = _widgetList[i + direction];
  }
  _widgetList[newIndex] = temp; 
}

```
Try to drag item: 1 -> 3, 7 -> 5

**Actual results:** 

```
I/flutter ( 8948): oldIndex: 1 newIndex: 4
I/flutter ( 8948): oldIndex: 7 newIndex: 5
```
![ReorderableView_result_wrong](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/25820430/81494921-06f05f00-92df-11ea-8f0e-2da1ff10902a.PNG)

I found when dragging item from up to down, ***"newIndex" is 1 increase*** from the actual result.

# 3. Special logic processing

In onReorder callback "newIndex" proactively subtract 1.

```
onReorder(int oldIndex, int newIndex) {
  print('oldIndex: $oldIndex newIndex: $newIndex');

  if (newIndex > oldIndex) {
    newIndex--;
    print('reset index: oldIndex: $oldIndex newIndex: $newIndex');
  }

  int direction = oldIndex < newIndex ? 1 : -1;
  var temp = _widgetList[oldIndex];
  var check = (index) => (newIndex - index).abs() + (oldIndex - index).abs() == (newIndex - oldIndex).abs(); 
  for (int i = oldIndex; check(i); i += direction) {
    _widgetList[i] = _widgetList[i + direction];
  }
  _widgetList[newIndex] = temp; 
}

```

**Expected results:** 

```
I/flutter ( 9602): oldIndex: 1 newIndex: 4
I/flutter ( 9602): reset index: oldIndex: 1 newIndex: 3
I/flutter ( 9602): oldIndex: 7 newIndex: 5
```

![ReorderableView_result_right](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/25820430/81494933-1bccf280-92df-11ea-8a66-06141457f04b.PNG)

# 4. View the source code of ReorderableView

View source code: [ReorderableListView](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/master/packages/flutter/lib/src/material/reorderable_list.dart) found the following comments.

```
Line 23 ~ 26
/// If [oldIndex] is before [newIndex], removing the item at [oldIndex] from the
/// list will reduce the list's length by one. Implementations used by
/// [ReorderableListView] will need to account for this when inserting before
/// [newIndex].
```

And some special instructions.

```
Line 405 ~ 410
void moveToStart() => reorder(index, 0);
void moveToEnd() => reorder(index, widget.children.length);
void moveBefore() => reorder(index, index - 1);
// To move after, we go to index+2 because we are moving it to the space
// before index+2, which is after the space at index+1.
void moveAfter() => reorder(index, index + 2);
```

# 5. In conclusion

This index is concerned with the position of the item during the dragging process to trigger the moved animation. It describes the index corresponding to the moved animation.

And what we focus on when we use it is from where to where drag, where the demand is relatively simple.

The former will do special treatment for subscripts, so I do n’t think that the processed subscripts should be passed directly to the user, and then the user must do a reverse process when using it.


